1
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.680
This video describes how to perform direct ophthalmoscopy.

2
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.620
Start with hand hygiene and introduce yourself.

3
00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:19.500
Confirm the patient's identity, then explain the examination and gain consent to perform it.

4
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.440
Take a few seconds to observe the peri-orbiter, eye-lens and

5
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.380
the eyes themselves, commenting on any positive or relevant negative

6
00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.320
findings.

7
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.480
The next step is to perform the fundal reflex test to assess for mediopacity.

8
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.880
Set the arc light by clicking the button three times for the brightest light,

9
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:39.760
and then move the lens rack to the top so there's no lens power.

10
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.373
With the arc light prepared, dim the lights in the room, and at arm's

11
00:00:44.373 --> 00:00:45.440
length, look through the

12
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:46.024
sight hole.

13
00:00:46.024 --> 00:00:50.400
Ask the patient to look at the light, observing both eyes at the same time.

14
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.800
The light should reflect from the fundus, back through the pupils, looking symmetrically bright

15
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.277
with similar colour.

16
00:00:56.277 --> 00:01:01.200
Any asymmetry suggests an abnormality such as corneal scar, cataract,

17
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.600
blood in the vitreous or a retinal tumour.

18
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.700
Next, examine the back of the eye.

19
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:06.300
Ask the

20
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.550
patient to fix on a target in the distance.

21
00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.800
Use your right hand and right eye to examine

22
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.200
their right eye and your left for their left.

23
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.600
Start with your feet close to the patient and

24
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.364
lean back.

25
00:01:16.364 --> 00:01:22.480
Identify the fundal reflex and follow this moving in on a path 10 to 15 degrees

26
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.980
temporal on the horizontal.

27
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.980
When you're close to the eye, the optic

28
00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.886
disc should come into view.

29
00:01:28.886 --> 00:01:33.840
Go in closer still and you'll get a wider and more stable view.

30
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.171
Comment on how distinct the margin of the disc is, the colour of the

31
00:01:37.171 --> 00:01:38.360
new retinal rim and the

32
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:39.612
cup to disc ratio.

33
00:01:39.612 --> 00:01:43.680
Then look for other signs along the four major branches of the retinal

34
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:43.948
vessels.

35
00:01:43.948 --> 00:01:48.240
Finish by asking the patient to look into the light to bring the centre of the

36
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.240
macula called the phobia interview.

37
00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.440
In a non-dilator dye, this will cause the

38
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.095
pupil to constrict and you will lose the view.

39
00:01:56.095 --> 00:01:58.160
In a dilated pupil, the phobia can

40
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.560
be seen as a slightly darker zone with a pinpoint reflection.

41
00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:03.360
Repeat this

42
00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.287
examination on the other eye.

43
00:02:05.287 --> 00:02:07.600
After the examination, thank the patient

44
00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.800
summarise your findings and repeat hand hygiene.

45
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.000
Please have a look at our other

46
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.400
University of St Andrews Art Flight project videos.
